# Docker build context exclusions.
#
# docker/search.Dockerfile builds from the repo root (it imports src/search.js),
# so this file IS load-bearing: it is what keeps that context to a few files.
# docker/web.Dockerfile has no COPY at all and compose points its context at
# docker/, so for that image this is defence in depth.
#
# A build context is uploaded to the Docker daemon WHOLESALE before the first
# instruction runs. data/ holds roughly 657 MB of documents, and 384 of them may
# never leave this machine (44 are commercial books under explicit no-reposting
# licences; see CLAUDE.md). If anyone ever adds a Dockerfile whose context is the
# repo root, without this file that upload would include all of them.
#
# The list is an ALLOW-LIST in spirit: deny everything, then re-admit the few
# things a build could legitimately need. An exclude-list fails open, and "fails
# open" here means shipping copyrighted material.
*

# Re-admit only what a build may need; each line is added by whoever adds the
# Dockerfile that needs it, with a reason.
#   docker/   web.Dockerfile's context (it COPYs nothing, but compose points there)
#   server/   the search service (docker/search.Dockerfile), whole
#   src/...   the four ES modules the service imports from the site: the shipped
#             ranker, its two imports and i18n.js's own import, store.js. Named one by one, never `!src/`, so the
#             page's own files (and anything added to src/ later) stay out.
!docker/
!server/
!src/search.js
!src/i18n.js
!src/paths.js
!src/store.js

# Belt and braces: even if the pattern above is ever widened to `!*`, these stay
# out. Docker applies later patterns last, so these must come after any re-admit.
data/
dist/
models/
.git/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
deploy.sh
